    description: "The `io-boundaries` subcommand produces a summary metric (`total_io_edges`) that is computed in two different ways depending on whether the user requests text or JSON output. The text-mode default path returns one count; the JSON-mode default path returns a different count (a different filtered sum, including categories that the text-mode path excludes). The `--include-tests` flag, intended to broaden inclusion, behaves monotonically in text mode (count goes up) and non-monotonically in JSON mode (count goes down). A third related metric in the JSON response — the sum of per-chain edge counts — is yet a different total. None of these match, and the relationship is undocumented.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) `--include-tests` inverts monotonicity between text and JSON modes.** Measured values: text default = 843 boundary calls; text `--include-tests` = 6432; JSON default `total_io_edges` = 7859; JSON `--include-tests total_io_edges` = 6432. The four numbers form the relation 843 < 6432 = 6432 < 7859. JSON default exceeds JSON `--include-tests` by 1427 — the opposite of the flag's intent. The text serializer applies `--include-tests` as a filter that monotonically increases the result set; the JSON serializer applies a different default-inclusion policy (`all edges with no filter` instead of `production-only`) so `--include-tests` actually narrows the result.\n\n**(2) Two different metrics are reported under the same field name `total_io_edges`.** Traced to `cli.py:3866-3886` plus `io_boundary.py:1249`. JSON default with `exclude_tests=True` falls into `cli.py:3870`'s `filtered_total = sum(len(e.chains) for e in filtered_entries.values())` — production-filtered chains INCLUDING the `external_potential` bucket: 7016 ep + 843 non-ep = 7859. JSON `--include-tests` falls into `cli.py:3882`'s else branch, which uses `bmap.to_dict()` and surfaces `bmap.total_io_edges = tagged_count` from `io_boundary.py:1249` — the count of boundary-tagged edges BEFORE `external_potential` is added: 6432. Same field name, two different code paths, two different semantic definitions.\n\n**(3) In the `--include-tests` JSON response, the sum of per-chain counts disagrees with the reported `total_io_edges` field within the same response object.** The `boundaries.chains` lists in that response sum to 25,128 (sub-totals: `external_potential`=18,696, `fs_read`=1593, `fs_write`=4395, `env_read`=152, `subprocess`=152, `logging`=125, others ≤10). But `total_io_edges` reports 6432 in the same response. A consumer summing the chains and a consumer reading the summary field get different numbers from the same JSON document.\n\n### Invariant\n\nA single named metric should represent one quantity regardless of request path. Inclusion flags should behave consistently across formats — the same flag should monotonically increase (or decrease) the count in both text and JSON mode. If multiple related metrics exist (e.g., \"total boundary edges\" vs \"sum of chain lengths\"), they should be named distinctly and their relationships documented in the response and the help text.\n\n### Scope\n\nEvery invocation of `io-boundaries` that consumes the metric. Magnitude of discrepancy between values is large (the three numbers — 843 / 6432 / 7859 / 25128 — differ by factors). The pattern is structural: same field name in three code paths produces three different definitions.\n\n### Exposure\n\n`io-boundaries` text output, `io-boundaries --json` output's `total_io_edges` field, `--include-tests` flag behavior.\n\n### Consequence\n\nConsumers using `total_io_edges` to track input/output edge volume get answers that depend on how they asked. Side-by-side comparisons across runs that use different paths are invalid. Programmatic consumers parsing JSON observe `total_io_edges=6432` while their own sum-of-chains reduce produces 25,128 from the same payload — trust in the metric is undermined when the summary disagrees with the contents of the same response object. Reproducible monitoring or CI gates on this metric are not feasible without picking one code path arbitrarily.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- Pick one canonical definition of `total_io_edges` (probably \"sum of chain lengths,\" since that's what the response actually contains in the populated form) and use it consistently across all code paths (text default, text `--include-tests`, JSON default, JSON `--include-tests`).\n- Have `--include-tests` apply uniformly across both serializers: the JSON default should match the text default (production-only baseline), and `--include-tests` should monotonically increase both. Decide which baseline definition is correct, and apply it before serialization.\n- Document the inclusion semantics in the help text and in the JSON response schema so consumers know what `total_io_edges` means.\n- Add a property test that asserts the sum of chain lengths equals `total_io_edges` for any JSON response (catches future drift between the summary field and the contents).\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-donij — which is deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # fbee
    fields:  # fbee
      dogfood_anon_id: "ITEM-b94123"  # fbee
      statement: "A single named metric should represent one quantity regardless of request path. Inclusion flags should behave consistently across formats. If multiple related metrics exist, they should be named distinctly and their relationships documented."  # fbee
      root_cause: "A subcommand produces a summary metric (a total count of input/output edges) that is computed in two different ways depending on the request. The text-mode default path returns one count. The JSON-mode default path returns a different count (a different filtered sum, including categories that the text-mode path excludes). A flag intended to broaden inclusion behaves monotonically (count goes up) in text mode and non-monotonically (count goes down) in JSON mode. A third metric on the JSON response, intended to be the sum of per-chain edge counts, is yet a different total. None of these match, and there is no documented relationship between them."  # fbee
